ams Business Administration Co-op Program Optional Arts and Science Internship Work Experience Programs Optional Master of Public Relations Internship Co-op & Internship Important Dates Student Resources Student FAQs Hire a Student Co-op & WIL Employer Centre Important Dates for Employer Partners Employer Resources Employer FAQs Co-op Student of the Year Awards Co-op Employer of the Year Awards Inspiring Stories Co-op in the Business Administration Program: “Worth Every Single Minute” An Optimistic Perspective on Tourism and Hospitality after COVID-19 Another Year of Mount Co-op Student Excellence Recognizing the Many Contributions of Mount Co-op Employers in 2020 A Journey Less Travelled: BPR Grad-Turned-Entrepreneur of Local Vintage Shop, Florence’s Collection “It’s all about the experience” – Tourism grad pursues her passion of creating customized, sustainable travel experiences Full Circle: From Co-op Student to Co-op Supervisor Innovation, Flexibility and Confidence: Celebrating the Contributions of Mount Co-op Students in 2021 Celebrating the Mentorship of Mount Co-op Employers in 2021 Keeping Up with the Mount Co-op Office Jump into the challenge: tried and true advice from an international BPR student Beyond the Work Terms: What to Expect When Walking Across the Stage and Into the Workforce Go for the Gusto: A Tourism Alum Reflects on the Journey Back to MSVU Another Year of MSVU Co-op and Work Integrated Learning on the Books (Literally!) When a connection becomes a career: a BPR student’s journey to post-grad employment From student to supervisor: one student’s upward journey at Future Inns Hotel Introducing a new internship option for MSVU’s Master of Public Relations Students Bridging Worlds: A Mi’kmaw Student’s Journey Toward Leadership in Indigenous Tourism Investing in Experience: How WIL Is Shaping Canada’s Workforce Future Soaring to New Heights – Finding Confidence and Clarity through Internship Taking my Degree Down Under A Work Term Abroad - Hannah Ghosn My Time with J.D. Irving Ltd. My Canadian Co-op Experience A Dream Come True: An Internship at Disney 12 Countries & 16 Cities – A Winter Exchange The Power of Perseverance Making Childhood Dreams a Reality Strong Women Work Here: National PR Firm Values Creativity, Collaboration and Fun PR student gains diverse communications experience and mentorship in federal government Tips and Tricks How to crush that cover letter Networking from Home During COVID-19 Tourism & Hospitality Management: The International Student Perspective A Seamless Transition: How to Successfully Wrap Up Your Work Term What to expect in your co-op journey – Answering your common co-op questions Common Co-op Challenges (and how to combat them!) Top Tips: Get the Most Out of Your Work Term Don’t Just Post a Job, Pitch It! How to Make Your Co-op or Internship Job Stand Out The importance of networking Understanding the Canadian Workplace Exploring entrepreneurship on a co-op Acing your interview The Employer Perspective Co-op and the Classroom Finding the co-op path that’s right for you Co-op in the time of COVID-19 Your go-to guide for online interviews Co-op & WIL | Grad Statistics Contact the MSVU Co-op & WIL Team Mission Statement Diploma & Certificates Library & Archives Research Services Library F.A.Q. Borrowing Services Borrow Anywhere Return Anywhere Student Study Carrels Student Study Rooms Library Food Bank Donations Printing Document Delivery Library Distance Services Library Services for Distance Courses Request/Update an ID Card (online form) Faculty Services Fair Dealing Policy E-Reserves/Online Reading Lists & Print Reserves Policy Request Form: Course Readings & Media (Reserves) Course Pack Information Library Liaisons Open Access Library Materials Recommendations Form Alumni Services Library Forms Library Instruction Library Research Orientaton Library Workshops Copyright & Fair Dealing About the Library Welcome Hours Location Strategic Plan Policies / Procedures LIBR 2100: Library Research Special Collections MacDonald Collection Lesbian Pulp Fiction Collection Call Number Arrangement Library Awards University Archives About Us Thesis Deposit Our Collections Policies Related Links Search Collections Microcredentials Case Management Fundamentals Ready to Lead Community Engagement: Meaning and Methods Frequently Asked Questions and Support for Current Microcredential Registrants Dr. Bernadette Russo Assistant Professor Contact Office: Seton 351 Phone: 902 457 6222 Email: bernadette.russo@msvu.ca Note: Dr. Russo is also associated with the Department of Cultural Studies . Background PhD in Social Justice, Literature, and Environment with emphasis in Indigenous Studies through the Department of English, Texas Tech University, Presidential Fellow, graduated 2020. Thesis: “Chasing the Wendigo: Contemporary Survivance and Sovereignty” Graduate Certification in Women and Gender Studies, Texas Tech University, graduated 2020. Master of Arts in English, Sam Houston State University, summa cu laude, graduated 2009. Thesis: ““Rippling Effects of Diaspora: (Dys)ease As a Function of Dislocation” Master of Science in Criminal Justice, Sam Houston State University, 36 hours (incomplete) Thesis: “Capital Punishment Contains No Restorative Value: A Restorative Justice Linguistic Analysis of 274 Final Statements of Death Row Inmates” Bachelor of Science, Northern Arizona University, magna cum laude, graduated 1998. Major: Criminal Justice; Minor: Anthropology Teaching For the current academic year (2026-2027), I will be teaching SOAN 2580 (Deviance) and SOAN 3582 (Law and Society) for the Department of Sociology/Anthropology. As I am temporarily cross-appointed, I will also be teaching three courses for the Cultural Studies Program this academic year. My teaching emphasizes a diverse range of perspectives and methodologies, particularly those engaging with decolonial, feminist, disability, and social justice issues. I strive to create an inclusive and accessible learning community within each of my courses with a strong eye toward Universal Design for Learning (UDL) practices. Research As someone of mixed ancestry with a very varied background, my research focuses on the many areas within social justice. My current projects include my monograph, Chasing the Wiindigoo Cannibal Spirit: Two Tropes and a Neoliberal Twist , an article regarding food deserts and reserves (Canada)/reservations (US) as colonial sites of oppression, and an article regarding the decolonial effects of palimpsestic multilingual audio-layering within film. Chasing the Wiindigoo Cannibal Spirit: Two Tropes and a Neoliberal Twist , under contract with the University of New Mexico Press. “A Manifesto of Indigenous Feminism? Stephen Graham Jones’s The Only Good Indians ,” under review with Studies in American Indian Literature . Indigenous Cannibal Spirits: A Database. An open access collection of popular culture Indigenous creations involving Indigenous cannibal spirits, launched June 26, 2026. https://indigenouscannibalspirtdatabase.ca/ “Deconstructing the Master’s House with His Own Tools: Code Switching and Double-Voiced Discourse as Agency in Gerald Vizenor’s The Heirs of Columbus ,” Studies in American Indian Literatures , Volume 29, Issue 4, 2017, pp. 58-75. DOI: https://doi.org/10.5250/studamerindilite.29.4.0058 “Shackles of a Distant Self and the Damning Complicit,” appearing in Madness, Women and the Power of Art and Forgiveness , edited by Frances Davies and Laura Gonzalez, Oxford, Inter-Disciplinary Press, 2013, pp. 181-196. ISBN-13: 978-1848882478 Co-Editor for e-book Beyond These Walls: Confronting Madness in Society, Literature, and Art , Probing the Boundaries Series, Oxford Inter-Disciplinary 2013. http://www.inter-disciplinary.net/probing-the-boundaries/ Database Website, Book Reviews and Encyclopedia Entries Review: The Buffalo Hunter Hunter by Stephen Graham Jones accepted and will appear in the open access journal Transmotion in the Fall 2026 issue. Review: Critical Indigenous Studies: Engagements in First World Locations edited by Aileen Moreton-Robinson appearing in American Indian Quarterly Volume 43, Issue 2, 2019, pp. 244-247. Review: Other Country: Barry Lopez and the Community of Artists by James Perrin Warren appearing in A rchivation Exploration , (Sowell Collection), volume 2, issue 1, 2017, online access. https://archivationexploration-ojs ttu.tdl.org/archivationexploration/index.php/ae/article/view/93/226 Review: Shaping America: The Supreme Court and American Society by Edward Mannino appearing in the Journal of American Culture , Volume 35, Issue 4, 2012, pp. 381-382. DOI:10.1111/jacc.12006_16 Encyclopedia Entry: “Historical Foundation of Parens Patriae ” appearing in Encyclopedia of Juvenile Justice , Sage Publications, Inc., 2003. DOI: https://doi.org/10.4135/9781412950640.n87 Home 166 Bedford Highway , Halifax, Nova Scotia, B3M 2J6 MSVU is located on unceded ancestral Mi’kmaq territory. Social Media » Connect with MSVU Facebook X Instagram YouTube Social Media myMount/email Moodle Intranet WebAdvisor Media Centre Parking & Security Campus Map © 2026 MSVU Disclaimer Website Feedback Sitemap"
    },
